// Whole numbers, such as `42`. Unsigned integers (`IntU`) hold 0 to 2^N - 1,
// and signed integers (`IntS`) hold -2^(N-1) to 2^(N-1) - 1, where N is the
// number of bits. Arithmetic that goes outside a type's range stops the
// program, and so does dividing by zero. Only signed integers can be negated,
// and only unsigned integers can be shifted, with `.<` and `.>`, by an `Int`,
// which stops the program if the amount isn't from 0 to N - 1, or if `.<`
// shifts off a `1`.
//
// Integers are equal if they hold the same value, and are ordered by their
// values. Their operators work on two of the same type, except that shifts
// are by an `Int`. Each integer type is in its own file, such as `IntU8` in
// `intu8`.

// The integer to use unless another is needed. Integer literals are `Int`s
// unless they're used as another integer type.
alias Int = IntS64
